 to the five modes of interior perception — spiritual analogues of the bodily senses — through which celestial beings receive influx. \"Living ambrosia\" and \"living air\" both denote the spiritual atmosphere that sustains wisdom-states: ambrosia as nourishing good, air as the medium of thought proceeding from the Divine. The phrase \"contests of the greatness\" (¶5) corresponds to the spiritual combats by which celestial states are confirmed and made one's own — temptation-struggles at the highest degree. The progression in ¶6 — from the Father through gods, rich ones, and angels to mountains, springs, and trees — traces the descent of influx from Divine Love through celestial goods into elevated states, thence into the origins of truth and finally into perceptions rooted in those truths: a complete arc of emanation from inmost love to outmost cognition.",
